# Prediction

**Prediction** is the derivation of expected observations from a history and a
model. It is the point at which a model commits itself to something that can
turn out false. In this vault the word covers two things: a consequence drawn
from a hypothesis, which is a step of reasoning, and a probability assigned to
the next symbol, which is what a language model computes. The discussion links
the second to compression and declines to equate either with intelligence.

| | Prediction as consequence | Prediction as probability |
|---|---|---|
| Comes from | a hypothesis, by [[deduction]] | a sequence model, by computation |
| Has the form | "if the model holds, this will be observed" | $P(x_t\mid x_{<t})$ |
| Is judged by | a test that confirms or refutes | the loss accumulated over a sequence |
| Lives in | the [[reasoning-cycle]] | [[compression-and-prediction]] |

## Description

### Lane one: a prediction is what a hypothesis is worth

In the discussion's table of operations, prediction takes a history and a
model and returns predicted observations. In the reasoning cycle it is the
product of the downward arc: [[induction]] or [[abduction]] proposes a model,
[[deduction]] unfolds it, and what comes out are predictions. Testing them is
the only contact the model has with anything outside itself.

This is the sense in which induction is never done alone. A hypothesis from
which nothing can be predicted cannot be wrong, and so cannot be supported
either. Prediction is the hinge between the two arcs.

### Lane two: a prediction is a code length

The question that opened the taxonomy of self-reference proposed a conjecture:
predictability is equivalent to compressibility, and compression is equivalent
to intelligence. The reply kept the part that is a theorem. For a
probabilistic sequence model the total code length of a sequence is
approximately its accumulated logarithmic loss,

$$L(x_{1:n})\approx-\sum_{t=1}^n\log_2 P(x_t\mid x_{<t}).$$

A model that predicts each next symbol well yields a short code, and a short
code yields a model that predicts well. That is a precise link between
predictive performance under logarithmic loss and code length under suitable
coding assumptions. It does not establish a universal equivalence of
predictability and compressibility, and it does not show that compression is
intelligence. The full argument belongs to [[compression-and-prediction]].

A language model is a predictor in this second sense by construction. Whether
it predicts in the first sense, by deriving consequences from a model it could
state, is a separate matter, and treating the two as one is the confusion the
two lanes are meant to prevent.

### Where prediction runs out

Prediction presupposes a shortcut: computing what will happen in less effort
than letting it happen. The discussion separates three ways the shortcut can
fail, which are easily run together.

- [[computational-irreducibility]]: there may be no substantial shortcut to
  the outcome of a computation other than running it.
- [[undecidability]]: no procedure answers the question for every case.
- Ambiguity: the requirements are incomplete, so there is nothing definite to
  predict.

None of these is escaped by using a language model, which is itself a
computational system. The essay draws the same conclusion when it rejects the
tempting formula that programs solve reducible problems and language models
irreducible ones.

There is also a limit of a different kind. A system that predicts its own
behaviour is a system that models itself, and the cycle drawn in the
discussion adds exactly this box: "build a model of the modeling process".
What such a self-model can and cannot contain is the subject of
[[reflection]].

## Relations

- It is one [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: instance-of}: history and model in, predicted observations out.
- It is produced by [[deduction]] {relation: uses} from a hypothesis that [[induction]] {relation: uses} supplied.
- It is the hinge of the [[reasoning-cycle]] {relation: part-of}.
- Its probabilistic form is one side of [[compression-and-prediction]] {relation: part-of}.
- It is checked by [[evaluation]] {relation: uses}, and a failed prediction triggers [[revision]] {relation: uses}.
- It is limited by [[computational-irreducibility]] {relation: contrasts}, where no shortcut to the outcome exists.
- It is limited differently by [[undecidability]] {relation: contrasts}, where no procedure decides every case.
- Turned on the predictor itself it becomes [[reflection]] {relation: generalizes}.
- It is not [[generation]] {relation: distinguished-from}: a prediction is a claim that can be wrong, a generated instance is merely new.
- It bears on the question [[ai-oriented-language]] {relation: answers}: what a language model can shortcut and what it cannot.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}, as one of the operations mapped there.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[compression-and-prediction]].** The link between a model's logarithmic
  loss and the length of the code it yields. It is the theorem that survives
  from the conjecture about intelligence.
- **[[reasoning-cycle]].** Observations, hypothesis, predictions, test. A
  prediction is the output of its downward arc and the input of its return.
- **[[computational-irreducibility]].** The absence of any shortcut to the
  result of a computation. It bounds prediction from one side, as
  undecidability does from another.
- **[[evaluation]].** Assessing a claim against a criterion. For a prediction
  the criterion is the observation that arrives.

## Queries

The relations declared on this card, as edges:

```hql
@[[prediction]] | uplinks | map(e => {to: e.target, how: e.data})
```

The cards that point here:

```hql
@[[prediction]] | downlinks | map(e => e.source)
```

Future lens that applies to this card: [[classify-operation]] would have to
tell a prediction in the first lane from mere continuation of a text.

